{"abstract":"The term digital money can be used to describe a wide variety of things, ranging from the use of phone credits to cryptocurrencies. Fundamentally, digital money distinguishes itself from cash in that digital money exists as data points, the record of which is kept and maintained by a variety of intermediaries. Thus, where paying with cash involves money changing from hand to hand, digital payment can be defined instead as a request to those intermediaries to edit the records of data. Drawing on literature from economic anthropology, and the more recent scholarship of digital payments and financial inclusion, this chapter explores the infrastructures of various forms of digital payment; what they mean for how value circulates; who owns them; when; and the role such payment gives intermediaries in governing digital transactions. These themes are explored through an empirical case of digital wallets in Indonesia, which examines how the language of proprietary platforms can obfuscate the power dynamics imposed through the digitalisation of transactions. Finally, the chapter discusses how the digitalisation of payments introduces specific challenges for ethnographic research, as well as the opportunities and limitations offered by taking an infrastructural approach.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Sandbukt, Sunniva"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383523"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Digital payments","Money","Peer to peer","Social infrastructure","Value"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"circuit board money an infrastructural perspective on digital payments","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/92559778/39_Circuit_Board_Money_Sandbukt_pre_print_copy.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383523","identifier":[{"id":"85160469368","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-9a473067-79c6-4efe-ba01-257c17c80a0c"],"keyword":["digital payments","money","peer-to-peer","social infrastructure","value"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-FD139D09C63CF2042E9EA6C587A8E9E4"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/92559778/39_Circuit_Board_Money_Sandbukt_pre_print_copy.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sunniva","key":"sandbuktpau","last":"Sandbukt","locid":"06d038a4-f659-4c53-8a6d-58e295ab603f","name":"Sandbukt, Sunniva","orcid":"0009-0002-4274-4991","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"757-775","publisher":"Palgrave Macmillan","status":"p","title":"Handbook of the Anthropology of Technology"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-9a473067-79c6-4efe-ba01-257c17c80a0c"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Circuit Board Money : An Infrastructural Perspective on Digital Payments","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383523","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"author_count":1,"contributors":["Sandbukt, Sunniva"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383952"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"imagineering ai the kinetics of integrating fantastical creatures in danish businesses","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-383952","ids":["itu-b7f73c46-3d7a-4bb0-b30e-eb74e5cdcca0"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-81852FFA9F501C8F6B87700552611FBC"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sunniva","key":"sandbuktpau","last":"Sandbukt","locid":"06d038a4-f659-4c53-8a6d-58e295ab603f","name":"Sandbukt, Sunniva","orcid":"0009-0002-4274-4991","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-b7f73c46-3d7a-4bb0-b30e-eb74e5cdcca0"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Imagineering AI: : The kinetics of integrating fantastical creatures in Danish businesses","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383952","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"In this article, we suggest approaching writing as a vulnerable practice marked by an unstable boundary between bodies: bodies of text and bodies of writers. We present an exercise-method that we refer to as Monster Writing, which we have developed in order to engage with these instabilities as well as in order to address experiences of difficulty, anxiety and uncertainty in relation with the text and writing process. Though the writing process can at times be exciting and thrilling, and at other times perhaps a little tedious and mundane, for some it also presents (more than) occasional encounters with one's own insecurities, shame and doubt. We argue that this potentially more painful relationship between writer and text should be awarded more attention in scholarship on writing, and that a way of doing so is through the framework of feminist theory on vulnerability, embodiment, and the monstrous.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Henriksen, Line","Kjær, Katrine Meldgaard","Blønd, Marie","Cohn, Marisa","Cakici, Baki","Douglas-Jones, Rachel","Ferreira, Pedro","Feshak, Viktoriya","Gahoonia, Simy Kaur","Sandbukt, Sunniva"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383081"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Technical University of Munich"],"keyword":["Feminist theory","Monster theory","Organizational studies","Vulnerability","Writing"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"writing bodies and bodies of text thinking vulnerability through monsters","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/gwao.12782","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383081","identifier":[{"id":"85121653378","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-7b7818dc-cf90-442f-826c-fe4264251f64"],"keyword":["feminist theory","monster theory","organizational studies","vulnerability","writing"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-5993E6636350380725B0F7E6033835DC"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/gwao.12782","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/gwao.12782","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"technical university of munich","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Technical University of Munich","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Line","key":"henriksenpau","last":"Henriksen","name":"Henriksen, Line","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Katrine Meldgaard","key":"kjærpau","last":"Kjær","locid":"0c46864b-a7f8-4439-aeda-0781f77bfe88","name":"Kjær, Katrine Meldgaard","orcid":"0000-0001-7994-5186","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Marie","key":"bløndpau","last":"Blønd","name":"Blønd, Marie","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marisa","key":"cohnpau","last":"Cohn","locid":"e9d14210-bc71-4824-b7cf-fd1448d022ab","name":"Cohn, Marisa","orcid":"0000-0002-3912-2166","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Baki","key":"cakicipau","last":"Cakici","locid":"b614ac6f-eefa-44a3-8b9a-092b88d86d37","name":"Cakici, Baki","orcid":"0000-0002-1039-8177","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rachel","key":"douglasjonespau","last":"Douglas-Jones","locid":"f11b0cc1-ad9f-4b12-b805-65b70e5bcd65","name":"Douglas-Jones, Rachel","orcid":"0000-0001-9562-6572","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pedro","key":"ferreirapau","last":"Ferreira","locid":"1b3cff7f-1549-4998-b52e-0ba9093e1d01","name":"Ferreira, Pedro","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Viktoriya","key":"feshakpau","last":"Feshak","name":"Feshak, Viktoriya","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Simy Kaur","key":"gahooniapau","last":"Gahoonia","locid":"6c543b1d-8137-489d-b444-d17921fc9de9","name":"Gahoonia, Simy Kaur","orcid":"0000-0001-9668-6868","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sunniva","key":"sandbuktpau","last":"Sandbukt","locid":"06d038a4-f659-4c53-8a6d-58e295ab603f","name":"Sandbukt, Sunniva","orcid":"0009-0002-4274-4991","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/gwao.12782","issn":["09686673","14680432"],"issn_org":["09686673"],"journal":"Gender, Work and Organization","publisher":"Wiley-Blackwell","status":"p","title":"Gender, Work and Organization"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85121653378"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-7b7818dc-cf90-442f-826c-fe4264251f64"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Writing bodies and bodies of text: Thinking vulnerability through monsters","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383081","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"In 2019, the founder of the Indonesian platform company ‘Gojek’ described how their app creates a ‘cyborg in our consumers’: using the digital infrastructure to summon goods and services, book on-demand drivers, and make digital payments using the integrated payment system. Implicitly, the app, phone, and driver tasked with doing things on your behalf all become part of your extended cyborg self. In this paper, I examine how platform companies such as Gojek implement digital infrastructures atop existing informal transactional practices rendering them visible for extraction for the consumer-cyborg, but also how this cyborg itself becomes a site of value extraction for the company. Firstly, I argue that the app overlays an existing social infrastructure, namely the loosely organised network of motorcycle taxis called ojek. Inserting itself as an interpretive interface, the company introduces stabilising parameters of exchange between drivers and their customers. Thus, they also position themselves as the access point to their consumer-cyborgs, a critical resource for drivers whose labour they now control and thus extract value from. Secondly, I argue that the privatised money tokens used within these digital ecosystems materialise the financial transactions themselves in the form of data traces. Replacing the transient exchange of cash between driver and customer, the consumer-cyborg also becomes a way for the platform companies to extract value from these temporary transactional constellations. This paper draws on ethnographic data collection through 6 months of fieldwork in Yogyakarta, Indonesia between 2018 and 2019. This fieldwork encompasses extensive interviews with both customers and service providers using Indonesian platform apps, as well as representatives from the companies themselves. Drawing on scholarship on social infrastructure and economic anthropology, this paper uses the concept of the ‘consumer-cyborg’ to draw attention to the extractive intersections of digital payments and the on-demand labour of the platform economy.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Sandbukt, Sunniva"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383364"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Digital payment","On demand labour","Platform economy","Social infrastructure","consumer cyborg"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"creating consumer cyborgs digital platforms as infrastructures for extraction","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-383364","ids":["itu-8e8b380b-0967-4f31-9a04-b820ddaa363e"],"keyword":["consumer-cyborg","digital payment","on-demand labour","platform economy","social infrastructure"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-061800243E6D27EB85326A9B5AD32713"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sunniva","key":"sandbuktpau","last":"Sandbukt","locid":"06d038a4-f659-4c53-8a6d-58e295ab603f","name":"Sandbukt, Sunniva","orcid":"0009-0002-4274-4991","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-8e8b380b-0967-4f31-9a04-b820ddaa363e"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Creating Consumer-Cyborgs : Digital Platforms as Infrastructures for Extraction","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383364","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Across the world, people are enlisted in the so-called ‘gig economy’ either as users or providers of digital services. In this thesis, I examine how digital ride-hailing apps in Indonesia, Gojek and Grab, have evolved from transport to a broader platform of financial services, including privately issued e-money, and how they advocate for the expansion of cashless peer-to-peer (P2P) payment systems. I investigate how their driver fleets are configured as an extended social infrastructure of the app environment and how their algorithmic labour management mobilises drivers to facilitate the use of digital payments by customers of the app. I argue that promoting the notion of peerhood by the companies both obfuscates how their apps configure users to participate in new hierarchical exchange relationships, as well as minimizes the companies’ role as intermediary. Since the circulation of money and data is where their profits are derived and the companies advertise themselves as providers of social equity, I further contend that this transactional constellation camouflages the financial interests that these companies have in generating platform activity. During six months of fieldwork in Yogyakarta, between 2018 and 2019, I explored these transactional dynamics by extensive use of the apps and by meeting both their drivers and customers, along with various fintech industry representatives and other local stakeholders. Through this work, I show that the exchange of digital money through these ride-hailing apps constitutes far more than the financial transaction and contains much more than can be summarized in an elegant acronym.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Sandbukt, Sunniva"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383691"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"top up with driver digital money transactional aspirations and peerhood in yogyakarta indonesia","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"doi","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109067502/PhD_Thesis_final_version_Sunniva_Sandbukt.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383691","ids":["itu-a60dca0c-7d24-4e50-8856-921b05dfbd2d"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-CA4F8CBFC7942ABA7E8E718A6E10F620"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109067502/PhD_Thesis_final_version_Sunniva_Sandbukt.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sunniva","key":"sandbuktpau","last":"Sandbukt","locid":"06d038a4-f659-4c53-8a6d-58e295ab603f","name":"Sandbukt, Sunniva","orcid":"0009-0002-4274-4991","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9788779490505"],"issn":["16023536"],"issue":"180","pages":"271","publisher":"IT-Universitetet i København","series":"ITU-DS","status":"p"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-a60dca0c-7d24-4e50-8856-921b05dfbd2d"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Top-Up with Driver : Digital Money, Transactional Aspirations, and Peerhood in Yogyakarta, Indonesia","type":"Other-Internet Publication","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383691","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"In 2015, the Indonesian start-up company, Go-Jek, launched a scooter-taxi app, modelled on Uber. Since then, the app has evolved to include multiple services, and there are a growing number of competitors with similar apps. These apps rely on maintaining a large fleet of drivers in order to provide their services. Though referred to as ‘mitra’ or partner in Indonesia, the relationship between drivers and platforms is decidedly asymmetrical. Drawing on empirical material collected through ethnographic fieldwork in Indonesia, this paper presents mechanisms that Go-Jek deploys within the app to manage their fleet of drivers. These include incentive tiers as well as deterrents for drivers servicing multiple apps. The paper then examines a variety of mechanisms deployed by drivers to navigate these affordances and restraints. Central to this is the practice of ‘account therapy’, by which drivers try to optimize the algorithms governing the distribution of customer orders. Having observed that some accounts receive more orders than others, drivers will ‘nurse’ a ‘sick’ account back into health with a variety of methods, effectively training their accounts to receive orders. Depending on these platforms as a source of income, the ability to care for your account becomes critical in ensuring consistent earnings. The paper demonstrates the inequalities of the tools available to the platform companies, and the people working for them, in adapting the app to better suit their needs. Drawing on literature from STS, this paper shows how practices of care become practices of resistance to intransparent algorithmic governance.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Sandbukt, Sunniva"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383178"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Algorithmic governance","CARE","Digital payments","Inequality","Platform economy"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"emergency care in the sharing economy the practice of algorithm optimization by indonesia s online taxi drivers","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-383178","ids":["itu-8210dacc-15aa-42df-8415-d6b8019f5e1e"],"keyword":["algorithmic governance","care","digital payments","inequality","platform economy"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-AA22D2D4C532BC2EDDA1AE80C75421B3"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT, European Blockchain Center (EBC)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sunniva","key":"sandbuktpau","last":"Sandbukt","locid":"06d038a4-f659-4c53-8a6d-58e295ab603f","name":"Sandbukt, Sunniva","orcid":"0009-0002-4274-4991","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-8210dacc-15aa-42df-8415-d6b8019f5e1e"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Emergency Care in the Sharing Economy : The practice of algorithm optimization by Indonesia’s online taxi drivers","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383178","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"While ‘peer-to-peer’ emerged as a technical term, growing traction means it is increasingly used to describe a wide variety of complex socio-economic transactions. Anthropologist Keith Hart observed that “A lot more circulates with money than the goods and services it buys. Money conveys meanings and these tell us how we make the communities we live in.” Arguably, the exchange of digital money also comprises far more than a simple financial transaction, and far more than can be summarised in an elegant acronym. Who are these so-called peers? Who are these (multiple) intermediaries, subtly obfuscated behind the ‘2’, and why do people rely on them? And what does disintermediation mean in such a context? This talk unpacks these questions, by drawing on ethnographic research on the use of digital payments in Yogyakarta, Indonesia. It analyses the various relationships between different types of users and the power and responsibility that the intermediaries have in configuring socio-economic dynamics when developing technology. The talk shows how ‘access’ to financial services and technologies is neither a binary nor static category. Rather, providing a viable technological alternative to conventional financial services means supporting people’s ability to transition flexibly between forms of digital and tangible money.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Sandbukt, Sunniva"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383372"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Digital payments","Intermediaries","P2P","Value","peerhood"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"peers and intermediaries in indonesia s p000000000002p economy","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-383372","ids":["itu-8ee2271a-e38a-49fc-9836-c2a221ca1b5a"],"keyword":["P2P","digital payments","intermediaries","peerhood","value"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-598A0807986DC38FE0A316D49FE46D42"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT, European Blockchain Center (EBC)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sunniva","key":"sandbuktpau","last":"Sandbukt","locid":"06d038a4-f659-4c53-8a6d-58e295ab603f","name":"Sandbukt, Sunniva","orcid":"0009-0002-4274-4991","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-8ee2271a-e38a-49fc-9836-c2a221ca1b5a"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Peers and Intermediaries in Indonesia’s P2P economy","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383372","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"author_count":1,"contributors":["Sandbukt, Sunniva"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383651"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Algorithmic governance","Digital payments","Indonesia","P2P"],"level":"com","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the money disappears at midnight accounts from life in the p000000000002p economy","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-383651","ids":["itu-a344443a-2f6b-4f9e-b203-a8f37a86bd43"],"keyword":["Indonesia","P2P","algorithmic governance","digital payments"],"lang":"English","level":"Commissioned","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-A4744AD8867D51B3FB51392BD70040F0"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT, European Blockchain Center (EBC)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sunniva","key":"sandbuktpau","last":"Sandbukt","locid":"06d038a4-f659-4c53-8a6d-58e295ab603f","name":"Sandbukt, Sunniva","orcid":"0009-0002-4274-4991","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-a344443a-2f6b-4f9e-b203-a8f37a86bd43"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Money Disappears at Midnight : Accounts from life in the P2P economy","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383651","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"The term ‘peer-to-peer’ (P2P) describes a technological infrastructure where two computer systems connect and share files without requiring a central server. The term is seeing increasing use within emerging financial technologies, where the peers have become people, and the integer ‘2’ signifies an intermediary technology facilitating an economic exchange. While the notion of a peer is typically deployed to invoke images of equality or socio-economic alignment, what does peerhood mean when the term P2P is used to describe services and technology ranging from Uber and international remittances to blockchain and Airbnb? In this paper, I explore peerhood through an empirical study based on ethnographic fieldwork conducted in Indonesia. Through the case of the digital wallet Go-Pay, I explore how the P2P wallet emerged as a socio-technical system with particular infrastructural and legislative conditions. I show how the intermediary company behind the wallet technology promotes an imagined peerhood between the different user groups of the app, and exemplify how the 2-hood of the economic exchange becomes evident in the various affordances and control mechanisms of the app. Thus, I make the argument that the promotion of the notion of peerhood obfuscates how the app actually configures its users to participate in an unequal relationship while minimizing the role of the intermediary. Finally, I detail how the same user groups co-opt the platform and enact their own variations of peerhood through collaborative practices.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Sandbukt, Sunniva"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384957"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Digital payments","Indonesia","Intermediaries","P2P","peerhood"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"partners who never meet an ethnographic perspective on peers and intermediaries in indonesia s p000000000002p economy","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-384957","ids":["itu-fad46e85-e276-4b90-83c8-03c2c60f4309"],"keyword":["Indonesia","P2P","digital payments","intermediaries","peerhood"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-7D0376688FD263DB8C2F7661CFBC1497"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT, European Blockchain Center (EBC)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sunniva","key":"sandbuktpau","last":"Sandbukt","locid":"06d038a4-f659-4c53-8a6d-58e295ab603f","name":"Sandbukt, Sunniva","orcid":"0009-0002-4274-4991","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-fad46e85-e276-4b90-83c8-03c2c60f4309"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Partners who never meet : An ethnographic perspective on peers and intermediaries in Indonesia’s P2P economy","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384957","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"This paper discusses how an ‘eco-system’ of taxi apps has taken part in changing what it means to be a taxi-driver in the Indonesian city of Yogyakarta. The relation between drivers and the companies behind the apps is described as a form of mutually ‘symbiotic’ interdependence. However, it is hardly a symmetrical relationship, nor one that is easily captured with the idealist notion of ‘peer-to-peer’ often used in IT-parlance. Among the concerns to the drivers is how they have begun to think differently about the way they are handling not only money but also mobility across the city-scape, when almost all interaction with passengers is facilitated digitally, and when their earnings are controlled and ‘cut’ by company-bank partnerships. The apps present various affordances from gamification to vouchers that incentivize both passengers and drivers, but some drivers try to operate in the gaps left open by the technology in order to access cash. The case demonstrates not only how the appropriation of such apps is understood within a context of multiple existing infrastructures, but also how value circulates in new forms and through new channels when mobility is reconfigured in the connection of the transport sector with app-based micro-finance.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Sandbukt, Sunniva","Dalsgaard, Steffen"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384763"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["App based micro finance","Digital mobility","Peer to peer dynamics","Symbiotic interdependence","Taxi apps"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the cut the circulation of money and taxis in yogyakarta indonesia","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-384763","ids":["itu-ed21fc30-4a06-4b4f-9301-0a422ec05c06"],"keyword":["App-based micro-finance","Digital mobility","Peer-to-peer dynamics","Symbiotic interdependence","Taxi apps"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-7E45F1F2A2CA74B1A3BA211F45691B4E"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT, European Blockchain Center (EBC)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sunniva","key":"sandbuktpau","last":"Sandbukt","locid":"06d038a4-f659-4c53-8a6d-58e295ab603f","name":"Sandbukt, Sunniva","orcid":"0009-0002-4274-4991","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Steffen","key":"dalsgaardpau","last":"Dalsgaard","locid":"bc7a60f7-45a0-401f-813f-6b4aa700c849","name":"Dalsgaard, Steffen","orcid":"0000-0003-3712-6988","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ed21fc30-4a06-4b4f-9301-0a422ec05c06"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Cut: The Circulation of Money and Taxis in Yogyakarta, Indonesia","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384763","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"During recent years, Nationally Appropriate Mitigation Action (NAMA) has become a key element of mitigation negotiations in the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC) process. At present, NAMA is considered as a key tool to be used by de¬veloping countries to structure and promote their potential emission reductions. Most countries are in the process of identifying NAMAs at national level.In order to select the NAMAs to be implemented first, many countries are willing to establish NAMAs prioritized list at national or sectoral level. A prioritization process will also be relevant as next year countries will have to translate their mitigation contribution outlined in their INDCs submitted to UNFCCC into implementation plans. Multiple-criteria decision analysis (MCDA) is a tool commonly used for decision making process, including to rank options or to short-list a limited number of options. This tool allows combining some criteria which are valued in monetary terms and others for which market values are not readily available in the economy.It allows the use of a full range of social, environmental, technical, economic, and financial criteria. MCDA is thus an appropriate tool to prioritize NAMAs in the context of national sustainable development, taking into account climate and development priorities. The objective of the publication is to enhance the knowledge of national stakeholders, in particular policy makers and decision makers, to understand the process for developing and applying a framework for NAMA prioritization (referred as NAMA Prioritization Pro¬cess). The publication will address the technical aspects related to the use of the MCDA tool and the institutional arrangements to implement an MCDA process, both in the context of NAMAs prioritization.Philippines is used as case example to illustrate the application of MCDA in a country-specific context.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Sharma, Sudhir","Desgain, Denis DR","Sandbukt, Sunniva"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-365657"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"nationally appropriate mitigation action developing a multi criteria decision analysis mcda process for prioritization of namas","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/128108848/2015_11_NAMA_Multi_Criteria_4thdraft.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-365657","ids":["dtu-cc94a8ca-935d-4f15-99af-f02e570835dc"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-DAD8B306C47B1F73C097504D6CA5B149"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/128108848/2015_11_NAMA_Multi_Criteria_4thdraft.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Management Engineering","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Management Engineering, UNEP DTU Partnership"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Sudhir","key":"sharmapau","last":"Sharma","locid":"9b7e6701-0d72-4bb2-8558-0aa65e1a7994","name":"Sharma, Sudhir","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Denis DR","key":"desgainpau","last":"Desgain","locid":"94a4ce86-147e-4d65-856c-2059a21e2aa9","name":"Desgain, Denis DR","orcid":"0000-0001-5